        "Bursts are groups of closely spaced tracer peaks separated by longer gaps of clean air. Murlis and colleagues compared their timing in field and forest recordings. Signal occupancy was about 20% in both environments, so equal occupancy hid large differences in how the odor arrived. Each burst therefore appears as one unit of odor input, and the spacing between units carries the information.",
        "Forest gaps between bursts lasted at least three times longer than field gaps. A burst contained about seven tracer spikes in the forest and about three in the field. The forest plume therefore clusters its encounters, so the same fraction of time containing signal arrives in fewer and denser episodes. Burst size and gap length together set how much odor a searching animal meets in each episode.",
        "Long clean-air gaps give a searching animal time to make movements before odor returns. A single mean concentration describes the average odor level, yet the forest plume delivers odor as clusters of about seven spikes separated by long intervals, and this temporal pattern is what the animal actually experiences. The timing of these encounters, rather than their average strength, decides whether the animal keeps moving toward the source.",
